{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":437355,"items":[{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202509.1087.v1","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI in Healthcare: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management of Open-Source and Proprietary Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6011-5080","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,12]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202509.1087.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare is defined by a critical dichotomy between open-source and proprietary models, a landscape further complicated by the emergence of autonomous Agentic Generative AI (AGI). This paper provides a comprehensive review and strategic framework to navigate this complex ecosystem. We analyze the technical capabilities, implementation challenges, and governance requirements of both AI paradigms through a systematic examination of current literature and emerging trends. Our findings indicate that while open-source models offer superior transparency, customization, and data privacy\u2014increasingly rivaling proprietary performance in diagnostics\u2014proprietary systems maintain advantages in reliability, support, and integration.    Concurrently, AGI introduces transformative potential for healthcare delivery through autonomous decision-making, predictive analytics, and workflow automation. These systems are projected to yield substantial cost savings and reduce diagnostic errors significantly. However, AGI also introduces complex risks ranging from algorithmic bias to regulatory fragmentation. Evidence shows concerning patterns in automated decision appeals and significant financial barriers to implementation that could limit accessibility.    To address these challenges, we propose a tiered risk-management and governance framework that synthesizes the strengths of both open and closed-source approaches. Our recommendations include the adoption of international certification protocols aligned with global explainability standards, federated learning architectures to ensure privacy while enabling collaboration, and adaptive policymaking to balance innovation with patient safety. The framework provides healthcare organizations with practical strategies for AI adoption while recommending policy directions for responsible deployment. This integrated approach aims to maximize the benefits of both open-source and proprietary AI while mitigating the unique risks posed by agentic systems, ultimately working toward more equitable, efficient, and safe healthcare delivery through artificial intelligence.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7009698","title":["AI Governance Framework for Large Language Models in Healthcare: Ensuring Trust, Privacy, Security and Regulatory Compliance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-0635-4419","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mudassir Ali","family":"Mohammed","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr Yohannes","family":"Tadesse","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7009698","abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, and Med-PaLM are transforming healthcare by enabling clinical decision support, patient engagement, and administrative automation. Yet, governance frameworks remain underdeveloped compared to traditional AI systems. This paper introduces the Enterprise LLM Governance Framework (ELGM\u00b2), a multi-layered model integrating lifecycle governance, prompt management, hallucination detection, human-in-the-loop validation, and compliance with global regulations (HIPAA, GDPR, EU AI Act, India DPDP Act, NIST AI RMF, ISO 42001). The framework embeds audit trails, explainability, and continuous monitoring to ensure trust, privacy, and accountability. By addressing the unique risks of LLMs, this research fills a critical gap and provides a roadmap for ethical and compliant adoption of generative AI in healthcare through 2030.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5469446","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI in Healthcare: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management of Open-Source and Proprietary Models"],"author":[{"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5469446"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6596080","title":["The AI Closed System Fallacy: How Legal Discourse Misidentifies Risk In Generative AI Governance"],"author":[{"given":"Susan","family":"Tanner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6596080","abstract":"<jats:p>This Article identifies and critiques what it terms the AI Closed System Fallacy: the widespread assumption that generative AI systems are primarily shaped by user inputs and that legal risk therefore flows mainly from prompting behavior. This assumption underlies ethics opinions, institutional policies, and regulatory guidance emphasizing confidentiality, client data input, and downstream outputs.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch13","title":["Ensuring Compliance and Ethical Standards with Generative AI in Fintech"],"author":[{"given":"Vishal","family":"Jain","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Archan","family":"Mitra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch13"},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3693-7452-8.ch016","title":["Navigating the Frontier of Generative AI Integration in Healthcare"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8925-7651","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Vikas P.","family":"Kaduskar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Electronics and Communication Engineering, College of Engineering, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed), Pune, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0181-903X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Harshada V.","family":"Mhetre","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Electronics and Communication Engineering, College of Engineering, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed), Pune, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Supriya M.","family":"Khatavkar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Electronics and Communication Engineering, College of Engineering, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed), Pune, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7209-8313","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Prashant A.","family":"Chougule","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Electronics and Communication Engineering, College of Engineering, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed), Pune, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0980-1336","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mangal V.","family":"Patil","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Electronics and Communication Engineering, College of Engineering, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed), Pune, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ravindra J.","family":"Moje","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electronics and Telecommunication Engineering, PDEA'S College of Engineering, Pune, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nithesh","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering, Manipal Institute of Technology, Manipal Academy of Higher Education, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,30]]},"container-title":["Advances in Healthcare Information Systems and Administration","AI Healthcare Applications and Security, Ethical, and Legal Considerations"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3693-7452-8.ch016","abstract":"<jats:p>This chapter delves into the potential impact of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare, including its influence on drug development, personalized medicine, patient care, and diagnostics. It also addresses ethical implications, regulatory challenges, privacy concerns, and the need for a robust governance framework. The chapter discusses governance models, regulatory bodies, industry standards, ethical considerations, fairness and equity in AI applications, and current laws and regulations governing AI in healthcare. It concludes by offering insights into emerging trends, future challenges, and actionable recommendations for responsibly integrating generative AI into healthcare.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.175825815.57896005\/v1","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6011-5080","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher Alumnus, International MBA, Bar-Ilan University"},{"name":"Touro College MSIT"},{"name":"Index Terms-Agentic AI, AI Governance, AI Risk Manage-ment, Autonomous Systems, AI Ethics, AI Safety, Regulatory Compliance, Multi-Agent Systems"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,19]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.175825815.57896005\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>The transition from Generative AI to Agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI) represents a fundamental shift in autonomous systems: from generating content to perceiving, reasoning, acting, and collaborating with minimal human input. This evolution unlocks significant economic potential, with estimates in the trillions of dollars, and introduces practical challenges across safety, security, ethics, and regulatory compliance. Evaluating current governance frameworks-including ISO\/IEC 42001 and the EU AI Act-we find significant gaps in addressing continuous monitoring, human-agent collaboration, and multi-agent coordination. Based on these findings, we propose a multi-layered governance framework that integrates technical safeguards, ethical alignment, and adaptive regulatory compliance. Key pillars include Governance by Design, automated risk detection, and human oversight to mitigate both technical and societal risks. Our analysis highlights that traditional AI governance frameworks, designed for static or narrow AI models, are inadequate for dynamic, multi-agent, goal-oriented systems. We systematically characterize Agentic AI by its core capabilities, multi-step decision-making processes, and architectural components. We identify a comprehensive risk taxonomy encompassing technical failure, adversarial exploitation, emergent unsafe behaviors, and accountability gaps. Evaluating current governance frameworks-including ISO\/IEC 42001 and the EU AI Act-we find significant gaps in addressing continuous monitoring, humanagent collaboration, and multi-agent coordination. Based on these findings, we summarize a multi-layered governance framework that integrates technical safeguards, ethical alignment, and adaptive regulatory compliance. Key pillars include Governance by Design, automated risk detection, and human oversight to mitigate both technical and societal risks. Our findings underscore the need for targeted policy interventions, operational best practices, and focused research on measuring and mitigating the realworld impact of Agentic AI. By bridging technical, ethical, and regulatory dimensions, this work provides actionable guidance for stakeholders aiming to deploy autonomous AI systems safely, responsibly, and effectively.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch11","title":["Ethical and Regulatory Compliance Challenges of Generative AI in Human Resources"],"author":[{"given":"Leena","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ankur","family":"Randhelia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ashish","family":"Jain","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akash Kumar","family":"Choudhary","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch11"},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003652649-12","title":["Integrating Generative AI and Learning Technologies to Redefine Insurance Risk Assessment and Training"],"author":[{"given":"Pooja","family":"Rani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Simarjeet","family":"Kaur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,2]]},"container-title":["Large Language Models, AI, and Workforce Transformation"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003652649-12"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5511060","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management"],"author":[{"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5511060"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4511540","title":["Large Language Models and Generative AI in Finance: An Analysis of ChatGPT, Bard, and Bing AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-7257-9141","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"David","family":"Krause","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4511540","abstract":"<jats:p>The field of finance is constantly evolving, and artificial intelligence (AI) is playing an increasingly important role in this evolution. Large language models (LLMs) and generative AI are two of the most promising AI technologies for the finance industry. This paper examines the application of LLMs and generative AI in the field of finance. It specifically focuses on three AI models: ChatGPT, Bard, and Bing AI. These models have shown significant potential in enhancing financial analysis, automating tasks, and providing valuable insights. By leveraging their language generation capabilities, they streamline financial analysis processes, improve decision-making, and advance natural language processing in finance. The analysis highlights the strengths and limitations of each model and emphasizes the importance of effective prompting and validation of AI-generated outputs. The evaluation of AI models in finance can be conducted using metrics such as ROUGE, BLEU, accuracy, precision, and perplexity. Despite their capabilities, challenges such as contextual understanding, factual accuracy, bias, fairness, and domain adaptation need to be addressed. Future directions for generative AI models in finance include domain adaptation, knowledge integration, explainability, interpretability, and ethical considerations. Generative AI models can be utilized responsibly and effectively in the finance industry; however, this will require careful consideration of the factors discussed in the paper.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5200768","title":["Large Language Scholarship: Generative AI in the Legal Academy"],"author":[{"given":"Alan Z.","family":"Rozenshtein","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kevin","family":"Frazier","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5200768"},{"DOI":"10.65150\/ep-jefrr\/v2e7\/2026-09","title":["Data Governance, Bias Mitigation, And Legal Risk: A Holistic AI Compliance Framework for U.S. Companies in High Stakes Sectors"],"author":[{"given":"Joy Oluchi","family":"Nwachukwu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Westcliff University, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Thaddaeuse","family":"Odhiambo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dorcas Akorkor","family":"Apaflo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Solomon Doe","family":"Adjaottor","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,20]]},"container-title":["Journal of Economic, Finance Research and Review"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.65150\/ep-jefrr\/v2e7\/2026-09","abstract":"<jats:p>This study examines data governance, bias mitigation, and legal risk within the context of a holistic Artificial Intelligence (AI) compliance framework for US companies operating in high-stakes sectors such as healthcare, finance, insurance, and critical infrastructure. The rapid adoption of AI systems has improved efficiency and decision-making capabilities. However, it has also introduced significant challenges related to algorithmic bias, lack of transparency, weak data governance, and increasing legal and regulatory exposure. Drawing on existing literature, the study highlights that inadequate governance structures and poor-quality datasets contribute to discriminatory outcomes, reduced accountability, and heightened compliance risks under evolving regulatory regimes. It further shows that algorithmic bias persists due to historical data inequalities and opaque machine-learning models, while legal frameworks such as privacy and anti-discrimination laws place additional obligations on organizations deploying AI systems. The study concludes that a holistic AI compliance framework integrating data governance, bias mitigation strategies, legal oversight, cybersecurity, and ethical accountability is essential for ensuring responsible and trustworthy AI deployment in high-stakes environments.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2147\/jmdh.s541271","title":["Ethical and Legal Governance of Generative AI in Chinese Healthcare"],"author":[{"given":"Jinrun","family":"Jia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shiqiao","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"container-title":["Journal of Multidisciplinary Healthcare"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2147\/jmdh.s541271"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_9","title":["Integrating AI Governance with Enterprise Governance Risk and Compliance"],"author":[{"given":"James","family":"Sayles","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Principles of AI Governance and Model Risk Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_9"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.frl.2025.107111","title":["Measuring firm-level supply chain risk using a generative large language model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0498-9114","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Siyu","family":"Fan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yifei","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-3640-1615","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ruochen","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,5]]},"container-title":["Finance Research Letters"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.frl.2025.107111"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/aic66080.2025.11212158","title":["Leveraging Generative AI and Natural Language Processing for Legal Risk Management Transforming Compliance, Contract Analysis, and Decision-Making in the Legal Domain"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6410-2969","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sai Krishna Reddy","family":"Mudhiganti","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,26]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE 4th World Conference on Applied Intelligence and Computing (AIC)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/aic66080.2025.11212158"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/b978-0-443-33124-4.00002-3","title":["Explainability in generative AI and large language models"],"author":[{"given":"Loveleen","family":"Gaur","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ajith","family":"Abraham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence and Ethics for Healthcare"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/b978-0-443-33124-4.00002-3"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icbdml68582.2026.11545173","title":["AI-Augmented Healthcare Communication: A Review of Large Language Model Healthcare Agents and Generative AI in EHR Integration"],"author":[{"given":"Shivam","family":"Tyagi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University Institute of Engneering Chandigarh University,Mohali,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Amit","family":"Kumar Goyal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University Institute of Engneering Chandigarh University,Mohali,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Deepti","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chandigarh University,Apex Institute of Engneering,Mohali,India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,27]]},"container-title":["2026 2nd International Conference on Big Data &amp;amp; Machine Learning (ICBDML)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icbdml68582.2026.11545173"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003539483-6","title":["Large Language Models in Healthcare Information Systems"],"author":[{"given":"M","family":"Sreenivasan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Abhay","family":"Narayan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,13]]},"container-title":["Generative Intelligence in Healthcare"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003539483-6"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394389131.ch4","title":["Regulatory Compliance and Data Governance in AI\u2010Driven Healthcare"],"author":[{"given":"S.S.","family":"Rahul","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Satish V.","family":"Kakade","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,9]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Neurology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394389131.ch4"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/b978-0-443-45252-9.00013-x","title":["Large language models and generative AI in healthcare: Multimodal intelligence, clinical integration, and the future of medical practice"],"author":[{"given":"Shubham","family":"Gupta","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Natural Language Processing for Healthcare"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/b978-0-443-45252-9.00013-x"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch8","title":["Innovation Unleashed Charting a New Course in Risk Evaluation with Generative AI"],"author":[{"given":"W.","family":"Shabeena Shah","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Khadeeja Bilquees","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"M. Jamal Mohamed","family":"Zubair","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch8"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781009492553.026","title":["Generative AI Systems and Corporate Governance, Compliance and Liability"],"author":[{"given":"Patrick J.","family":"O\u2019Malley","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,7]]},"container-title":["The Cambridge Handbook of Generative AI and the Law"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781009492553.026"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch18","title":["Insurance Disruption"],"author":[{"given":"Swati","family":"Gupta","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ruchika","family":"Rastogi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch18"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/healthcare11202776","title":["Leveraging Generative AI and Large Language Models: A Comprehensive Roadmap for Healthcare Integration"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7910-9396","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ping","family":"Yu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing and Information Technology, University of Wollongong, Wollongong, NSW 2522, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hua","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Section of Biomedical Informatics and Data Science, Yale School of Medicine, 100 College Street, Fl 9, New Haven, CT 06510, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xia","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science, Rice University, P.O. Box 1892, Houston, TX 77251-1892, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1147-5741","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chao","family":"Deng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Medical, Indigenous and Health Sciences, University of Wollongong, Wollongong, NSW 2522, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023,10,20]]},"container-title":["Healthcare"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/healthcare11202776","abstract":"<jats:p>Generative artificial intelligence (AI) and large language models (LLMs), exemplified by ChatGPT, are promising for revolutionizing data and information management in healthcare and medicine. However, there is scant literature guiding their integration for non-AI professionals. This study conducts a scoping literature review to address the critical need for guidance on integrating generative AI and LLMs into healthcare and medical practices. It elucidates the distinct mechanisms underpinning these technologies, such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLFH), including few-shot learning and chain-of-thought reasoning, which differentiates them from traditional, rule-based AI systems. It requires an inclusive, collaborative co-design process that engages all pertinent stakeholders, including clinicians and consumers, to achieve these benefits. Although global research is examining both opportunities and challenges, including ethical and legal dimensions, LLMs offer promising advancements in healthcare by enhancing data management, information retrieval, and decision-making processes. Continued innovation in data acquisition, model fine-tuning, prompt strategy development, evaluation, and system implementation is imperative for realizing the full potential of these technologies. Organizations should proactively engage with these technologies to improve healthcare quality, safety, and efficiency, adhering to ethical and legal guidelines for responsible application.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6567798","title":["An Enterprise AI Operating Model for Regulated Pharmaceutical Organizations: Governance, Risk, and Scalable Implementation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9627-7607","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Viqas","family":"Shafi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6567798","abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;\n Artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in pharmaceutical organizations is rapidly moving beyond isolated pilot initiatives toward broader enterprise deployment. However, regulated environments impose requirements related to patient safety, data integrity, inspection readiness, and jurisdiction-specific compliance that render generic AI operating models insufficient. In practice, many organizations encounter a persistent scaling gap: locally successful AI initiatives fail to industrialize because governance structures, validation approaches, and accountability mechanisms are not designed for probabilistic, evolving systems deployed in regulated workflows.\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n &lt;br&gt;\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n &amp;nbsp;This paper proposes a conceptual enterprise AI operating model tailored to regulated pharmaceutical organizations. Using a framework development approach, the study synthesizes principles from regulated quality systems, risk-based governance, and AI lifecycle oversight to articulate a governance-driven model for scalable implementation. The proposed framework is organized around five interdependent layers: strategic governance, risk and compliance controls, enterprise enablement, use case delivery, and human oversight and escalation. In parallel, the paper reframes validation for AI as lifecycle assurance rather than point-in-time verification, emphasizing risk stratification, continuous reassurance , and structured accountability for human review.&amp;nbsp;\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n &lt;br&gt;\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n The paper contributes a conceptual architecture for governing AI in regulated pharma that is neither purely technical nor purely policy-oriented. Instead, it positions governance as trust infrastructure: a reusable organizational capability that enables responsible AI scale while preserving the primacy of patient safety, scientific defensibility, and regulatory compliance. The framework offers a defensible middle path between ungoverned experimentation and excessively restrictive controls, and it provides a foundation for future empirical work on implementation and assurance in real-world pharmaceutical settings.\n&lt;\/div&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781118269213.ch42","title":["Insurance Industry and Solvency II"],"author":[{"given":"Anthony","family":"Tarantino","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2008,2,29]]},"container-title":["Governance, Risk, and Compliance Handbook"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781118269213.ch42"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11390955","title":["Responsible AI to AI Governance: An Awareness-Driven, Culture-Conditioned Model in Healthcare"],"author":[{"given":"Omer","family":"Aftab Qureshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Abu Dhabi University,Adjunct Faculty Marketing and AI,Abu Dhabi,United Arab Emirates"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dr.","family":"Loubna Saad Saoud","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The British University in Dubai,Dubai,United Arab Emirates"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"container-title":["2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11390955"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-88988-2_9","title":["Generative Large Language Models in Clinical, Legal and Financial Domains"],"author":[{"given":"Geetanjali","family":"Garg","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Shobha","family":"Bhatt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Transformative Natural Language Processing"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-88988-2_9"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5_1","title":["Introduction to Generative AI and Large Language Models (LLMs)"],"author":[{"given":"Banu","family":"Parasuraman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Mastering Spring AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5_1"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7154211","title":["Generative AI in Biomedical and Healthcare Applications A Comprehensive Review of Large Language Models and Cloud Integration"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6830-5930","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Sachin","family":"Saxena","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akanksha","family":"Saxena","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7154211","abstract":"<jats:p>In this paper, we present a thorough review of GenAI, covering its fundamental concepts, latest advancements, and current state-of-the-art developments. We take a close look at leading language models, examining factors like model size, batch configuration, dataset complexity, and accuracy. Notably, Flan-PaLM excels in large-scale applications, while SciBERT demonstrates strong performance in scientific domains. Biomedical models such as BioGPT and Med-PaLM highlight the importance of domain-specific architectures for achieving high accuracy. The wide range of model performance and complexity underscores the need for careful selection when tackling specialized biomedical tasks. We also compare Claude\u2019s Opus 4.7, Sonnet 4.6, and Haiku 4.5 models, illustrating the trade-offs between computational capability and cost. Opus stands out for complex scenarios with its large context and output window, Haiku offers a cost-effective option for high-volume or real-time tasks, and Sonnet provides a balanced alternative. Additionally, we discuss how advanced language models are being integrated with major cloud platforms, enabling practical applications in bioinformatics, healthcare operations, disease coding, and data management. Our study further explores Claude\u2019s effectiveness in automating clinical trial analysis, speeding up drug discovery, and supporting literature review, all of which promote evidence-based decisions in life sciences. Finally, our evaluation of clinical tasks demonstrates that these models can accurately characterize diseases and reliably monitor healthcare quality.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.174291179.92552739\/v1","title":["AI-Driven Mental Healthcare 5.0: A Survey of Opportunities and Challenges in Leveraging Large Language Models and Generative AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4263-7168","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Akshi","family":"Kumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Goldsmiths University of London Department of Computing"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Karan","family":"Gupta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delhi Technological University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kartik","family":"Verma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delhi Technological University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8951-5996","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sanjay","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Delhi Technological University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,25]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.174291179.92552739\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Generative AI into\nmental healthcare is reshaping diagnostic, therapeutic, and support\nsystems, marking a transition toward AI-driven mental healthcare. This\npaper explores various methodologies for incorporating LLMs into mental\nhealth applications, categorizing research into key strategies such as\nprompt engineering, fine-tuning, emotional theory integration,\nAI-powered chatbots, and long-term memory architectures. Through case\nstudies, we evaluate their effectiveness in areas like depression\ndetection, suicide risk assessment, and AI-assisted psychological\ncounseling, highlighting both their potential benefits and associated\nchallenges. While LLMs enable more empathetic interactions, personalized\ninterventions, and early risk identification, concerns remain regarding\nbias, misinformation, safety risks, and ethical issues related to\nprivacy, informed consent, and AI\u2019s role in clinical decision-making. We\noutline critical research gaps, stressing the importance of real-world\nvalidation, standardized evaluation methods, and strong risk mitigation\nframeworks. This paper serves as a guide for the responsible, scalable,\nand ethical integration of AI in mental healthcare, ensuring these\ntechnologies enhance patient well-being while adhering to clinical and\nethical standards.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.55524\/ijircst.2026.14.1.12","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI in Healthcare: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management of Open-Source and Proprietary Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6011-5080","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Innovative Research in Engineering and Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.55524\/ijircst.2026.14.1.12","abstract":"<jats:p>This paper provides a comprehensive review and strategic framework to navigate this complex ecosystem of open-source and proprietary models for healthcare. We analyze the technical capabilities, implementation challenges, and governance requirements of both AI paradigms through a systematic and organnized survey of current literature and emerging trends. Our findings indicate that while open-source models offer superior transparency, customization, and data privacy\u2014increasingly rivaling proprietary performance in diagnostics\u2014proprietary systems maintain advantages in reliability, support, and integration. However, AGI also introduces complex risks ranging from algorithmic bias (if uncontrolled) to regulatory fragmentation (lack of regulation). Evidence shows concerning patterns in automated decision appeals and significant financial barriers to implementation that could limit accessibility. To address these challenges, we propose a tiered risk-management and governance framework that synthesizes the strengths of both open and closed-source approaches. Our recommendations include the adoption of international certification protocols aligned with global explainability standards, federated learning architectures to ensure privacy while enabling collaboration, and adaptive policymaking to balance innovation with patient safety. This integrated approach aims to maximize the benefits of both open-source and proprietary AI while focusing on remodification of unique risks posed by agentic systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1154-8_7","title":["AI Governance and Responsible AI"],"author":[{"given":"Arindam","family":"Ganguly","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Scaling Enterprise Solutions with Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1154-8_7"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-981-96-6448-1_13","title":["Legal and Compliance Risks of AI and Blockchain Technologies"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6556-0269","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Merve Ayseg\u00fcl","family":"Kulular","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3348-6353","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mahmut Furkan","family":"Balaban","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Accounting, Finance, Sustainability, Governance &amp; Fraud: Theory and Application","Futurisks: Risk Management in the Digital Age"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-981-96-6448-1_13"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icctwc68241.2026.11583626","title":["Governance-as-Architecture: A Framework for Compliance-Native AI in Regulated Large-Scale Data Ecosystems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9728-0207","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sudhir","family":"Vishnubhatla","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher,Tampa,Florida,USA,34638"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,1]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Computing Theory and Wireless Communications (ICCTWC)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icctwc68241.2026.11583626"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5652350","title":["The Agentic AI Governance Framework: A Universal Model for Risk, Accountability, and Compliance in Autonomous Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8440-2191","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rajat","family":"Pandey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5652350","abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid and enthusiastic interest in development and deployment of Agentic AI and especially autonomous agents which are capable of independent reasoning, leveraging and&amp;nbsp; executing tools, has outpaced the definition of governance mechanism that is required to manage their operational, ethical and regulatory risks.There are existing AI governance models like NIST AI RMF, ISO\/IEC 42001, do provide foundational controls however, they are very generic and lack the implementation depth for these conitnuously acting, multi-agent systems. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This paper introduces The Agentic AI Governance Framework, which is a six principled lifecycle model designed to ensure traceability, accountability, and regulatory alignment across agentic deployments. The framework formalizes quantifiable controls through two applied mechanisms: the Agentic Log Retention Index (ALRI), which defines evidenceretention periods based on agent risk and jurisdiction, and a Runtime Governance Architecture integrating human-in-loop escalation, tool-access allow-lists, and modelprovenance capture.&amp;nbsp;Implementation mappings are provided for major orchestration environments including LangChain, Power Platform, and UiPath.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Adoption of the framework enables organizations to reconstruct agentic decisions within 24 hours, achieve \u2265 95 % provenance coverage, and detect behavioral drift within 10 % variance. The proposed model bridges the current gap between compliance principles and deployable governance practice, establishing a reusable baseline for safe and auditable Agentic AI operations.&lt;br&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.32388\/a8dyj7.2","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments using DALL.E: Generative AI-Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,28]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/a8dyj7.2","abstract":"<jats:p>This research investigated the role of artificial intelligence (AI), specifically the DALL.E model by OpenAI, in advancing data generation and visualization techniques in agriculture. DALL.E, an advanced AI image generator, works alongside ChatGPT's language processing to transform text descriptions and image clues into realistic visual representations of the content. The study used both approaches of image generation: text-to-image and image-to-image (variation). Two types of datasets depicting fruit crop environment and \u201ccrop-vs-weed\u201d environment were generated. These AI-generated images were then compared against ground truth images captured by sensors in real agricultural fields. The comparison was based on Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Feature Similarity Index (FSIM) metrics. For fruit crops, image-to-image generation exhibited a 5.78% increase in average PSNR over text-to-image methods, signifying superior image clarity and quality. However, this method also resulted in a 10.23% decrease in average FSIM, indicating a diminished structural and textural similarity to the original images. Conversely, in crop vs weed scenarios, image-to-image generation showed a 3.77% increase in PSNR, demonstrating enhanced image precision, but experienced a slight 0.76% decrease in FSIM, suggesting a minor reduction in feature similarity. Similar to these measures, human evaluation also showed that images generated using image-to-image-based method were more realistic compared to those generated with text-to-image approach. The results highlighted DALL.E's potential in generating realistic agricultural image datasets and thus accelerating the development and adoption of precision agricultural solutions.\n<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0013472600003956","title":["A Review on Large Language Models and Generative AI in Banking"],"author":[{"given":"Daniel","family":"Staegemann","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Magdeburg Research and Competence Cluster VLBA, Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg, Magdeburg, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Christian","family":"Haertel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Magdeburg Research and Competence Cluster VLBA, Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg, Magdeburg, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Christian","family":"Daase","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Magdeburg Research and Competence Cluster VLBA, Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg, Magdeburg, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Matthias","family":"Pohl","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Data Science, German Aerospace Center (DLR), Jena, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mohammad","family":"Abdallah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Software Engineering, Al-Zaytoonah University of Jordan, Amman, Jordan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Klaus","family":"Turowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Magdeburg Research and Competence Cluster VLBA, Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg, Magdeburg, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 7th International Conference on Finance, Economics, Management and IT Business"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0013472600003956"},{"DOI":"10.52133\/ijrsp.v7.80.2","title":["Electronic Governance Controls for AI-Enabled Agentic Large Language Models in Digital Government (Compliance-by-Design and Evidence-Linked Evaluation)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8106-078X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dr. Maram","family":"Almaghrabi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Assistant professor, Data Science Department, College of Computing, Umm Al-Qura University, Makkah, Saudi Arabia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,20]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Research and Studies Publishing"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52133\/ijrsp.v7.80.2","abstract":"<jats:p>Artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs) and agentic AI systems, is increasingly used to modernize digital government, yet public-sector deployment must satisfy accountability, privacy, and auditability requirements. This paper aims to propose an electronic-governance framework that enables governed use of LLM-enabled agentic systems under binding constraints. Through qualitative documentary analysis, the study examines policy and governance evidence across digital-government strategies, data-governance and privacy instruments, and public-sector AI initiatives. Empirical grounding is strengthened through triangulation with official digital-government maturity indicators, a flagship platform vignette illustrating operational scalability, and a reproducible open-data key performance indicator (KPI) baseline derived from a government complaint-escalation series. The study concludes that scalable public value depends on bounded autonomy implemented through allowlisted tools, controlled state and memory, verification and feedback loops, role-based access control, audit logging, and human-in-the-loop gating. The paper contributes an evaluation-ready input\u2013process\u2013output framework to assess productivity, decision quality, and auditable compliance aligned with Vision 2030 and SDAIA-led transformation, The paper further strengthens feasibility claims through official maturity indicators and flagship implementation evidence, showing advanced service-experience maturity and institutional transformation readiness that support scaling LLM-enabled service augmentation and governance-constrained agentic execution under regulated constraints.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/su17219643","title":["Parametric Insurance for Sustainable Disaster Risk Finance: Legal, Data, and Governance Pathways in Slovenia and Croatia"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-5344-5105","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nina","family":"Pleterski","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"European Faculty of Law, New University, Delpinova Ulica 18B, 5000 Nova Gorica, Slovenia"},{"name":"Pleterski Puhari\u010d Law Firm Ltd., Dunajska Cesta 156, 1000 Ljubljana, Slovenia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,30]]},"container-title":["Sustainability"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/su17219643","abstract":"<jats:p>Disasters caused by natural hazards, including the August 2023 floods in Slovenia and the 2020 earthquakes in Croatia, resulted in a combined damage and loss of about EUR 26 billion. Indemnity insurance covered only a small share, shifting recovery to public budgets. This review examines whether parametric insurance can provide transparent, pre-arranged, and auditable post-event liquidity to smooth public finances and support timely recovery. A structured qualitative review of peer-reviewed studies, supervisory materials, and EU and national law assesses data readiness, enforceability, and consumer protection duties. EU rules address parts of prudential and conduct risk. However, gaps persist in trigger verification, automated execution, and in the treatment of third-party trigger data sources and calculation methodologies documented for supervisory reviews and audits (no published parametric-specific accreditation standards). The core gap reflects the low take-up of catastrophe insurance rather than a low overall insurance penetration. Parametric cover is treated strictly as a complement to indemnity insurance. We outline narrowly scoped pilots using verifiable, publicly sourced triggers, version-controlled calculations, pre-tested basis risk disclosures, and reversible, auditable settlements with human oversight. Parametric designs add value only when verifiable triggers, transparent disclosures, and supervisory audits are embedded ex ante.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6618465","title":["Asynchronous Compliance in AI Governance: Rules in Multiple Legal States"],"author":[{"given":"Oswaldo","family":"Maxwell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6618465","abstract":"<jats:p>Artificial intelligence governance increasingly requires organizations to make design, procurement, and deployment decisions under rules that are neither fully settled nor fully absent. Existing accounts often describe this environment in terms of regulatory lag, fragmented standards, or generalized uncertainty. This Article advances a narrower claim. It identifies a distinct governance conditiondescribed here as asynchronous compliance-in which organizations build and deploy systems under rules distributed across multiple legal states at once: proposed but not final, finalized but not yet in force, in force but not yet enforced in any stable pattern, enforced but not yet interpreted consistently, and active elsewhere under jurisdictionally non-fungible compliance logics. Once obligations are distributed across these overlapping states, compliance can no longer be treated as a singular point-in-time status. Organizations instead optimize for optionality under legal incompletion. The practical result is not merely uncertainty, but a recognizable pattern of organizational behavior: architectural conservatism, documentary branching, procurement caution, segmented release, and the movement of governance authority upstream into design and deployment decisions. In that environment, governance tractability becomes a competitive variable, and technically stronger systems can lose to weaker systems that are easier to defend administratively. The contribution is intentionally diagnostic. It does not propose a harmonization strategy, a preferred enterprise compliance architecture, or an institutional reform agenda. Its narrower objective is to clarify why the contemporary governance difficulty lies not simply in incomplete rules, but in the fact that incomplete rules already govern.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch12","title":["Navigating the Frontier of Finance"],"author":[{"given":"Ahmad","family":"Haidar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ahmad","family":"Abbass","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch12"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.175616948.85236631\/v1","title":["Dual-Use of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Generative AI (GenAI) in Cybersecurity: Risks, Defenses, and Governance Strategies"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4058-1108","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kiarash","family":"Ahi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"LLM\/ AI Product Leader"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vaibhav","family":"Agrawal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lead Security Engineer"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saeed","family":"Valizadeh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lead Security Researcher, Virelya Intelligence Research Labs"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,26]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.175616948.85236631\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) are profoundly reshaping the cybersecurity landscape, creating a dual-use dilemma where the same capabilities that enhance defenses also empower sophisticated offensive threats. While security teams leverage LLMs for automated threat detection, static code analysis, and DevSecOps integration, adversaries weaponize them to generate polymorphic malware, zero-day exploits, and highly scalable social engineering payloads. This survey is the first to systematically unify these offensive and defensive perspectives. Drawing on a comprehensive analysis of over 80 academic, industry, and threat intelligence sources, we project the share of LLM-assisted malware to rise from an estimated 2% of all attacks in 2021 to 50% by 2025. We synthesize key countermeasures from platforms like Google Play Protect and Microsoft Security Copilot, and we propose a robust governance roadmap that addresses the technical, ethical, and policy challenges of secure, privacypreserving, and scalable LLM deployment. Our findings position LLMs not merely as tools, but as critical infrastructure-central to both attack and defense-in the emerging AI-powered cybersecurity era. Impact Statement-This work delivers the first comprehensive framework uniting offensive and defensive perspectives on Large Language Models (LLMs) in cybersecurity. By combining technical strategies with governance requirements, it provides actionable guidance for integrating LLMs into secure development, automated threat detection, and policy enforcement. The proposed roadmap enables governments, enterprises, and security teams to deploy LLMs in ways that are scalable, privacy-preserving, and resilient against adversarial misuse. Its synthesis of cross-industry case studies, risk projections, and regulatory trends equips decision-makers with a blueprint for mitigating dual-use risks while maximizing the societal and economic benefits of AI-driven security innovation.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/gaclm67198.2025.11232411","title":["AI-Powered Multi-Hazard Alerting System for Predictive Risk Management in Public Safety and Insurance"],"author":[{"given":"Balaji","family":"Chode","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Unified Business Technologies Inc,Troy,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,18]]},"container-title":["2025 2nd International Generative AI and Computational Language Modelling Conference (GACLM)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/gaclm67198.2025.11232411"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.67363","title":["Generative Large Language Model\u2014Powered Conversational AI App for Personalized Risk Assessment: Case Study in COVID-19 (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-2419-3101","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad Amin","family":"Roshani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0776-8045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiangyu","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2995-3385","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yao","family":"Qiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1717-7593","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Srinivasan","family":"Suresh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9579-6798","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Steven","family":"Hicks","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5183-0325","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Usha","family":"Sethuraman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0225-7817","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongxiao","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,9]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.67363","abstract":"<sec>\n                    <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                        <p>Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated powerful capabilities in natural language tasks and are increasingly being integrated into health care for tasks like disease risk assessment. Traditional machine learning methods rely on structured data and coding, limiting their flexibility in dynamic clinical environments. This study presents a novel approach to disease risk assessment using generative LLMs through conversational artificial intelligence (AI), eliminating the need for programming.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                        <p>This study evaluates the use of pretrained generative LLMs, including LLaMA2-7b and Flan-T5-xl, for COVID-19 severity prediction with the goal of enabling a real-time, no-code, risk assessment solution through chatbot-based, question-answering interactions. To contextualize their performance, we compare LLMs with traditional machine learning classifiers, such as logistic regression, extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and random forest, which rely on tabular data.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                        <p>We fine-tuned LLMs using few-shot natural language examples from a dataset of 393 pediatric patients, developing a mobile app that integrates these models to provide real-time, no-code, COVID-19 severity risk assessment through clinician-patient interaction. The LLMs were compared with traditional classifiers across different experimental settings, using the area under the curve (AUC) as the primary evaluation metric. Feature importance derived from LLM attention layers was also analyzed to enhance interpretability.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                        <p>Generative LLMs demonstrated strong performance in low-data settings. In zero-shot scenarios, the T0-3b-T model achieved an AUC of 0.75, while other LLMs, such as T0pp(8bit)-T and Flan-T5-xl-T, reached 0.67 and 0.69, respectively. At 2-shot settings, logistic regression and random forest achieved an AUC of 0.57, while Flan-T5-xl-T and T0-3b-T obtained 0.69 and 0.65, respectively. By 32-shot settings, Flan-T5-xl-T reached 0.70, similar to logistic regression (0.69) and random forest (0.68), while XGBoost improved to 0.65. These results illustrate the differences in how generative LLMs and traditional models handle the increasing data availability. LLMs perform well in low-data scenarios, whereas traditional models rely more on structured tabular data and labeled training examples. Furthermore, the mobile app provides real-time, COVID-19 severity assessments and personalized insights through attention-based feature importance, adding value to the clinical interpretation of the results.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                        <p>Generative LLMs provide a robust alternative to traditional classifiers, particularly in scenarios with limited labeled data. Their ability to handle unstructured inputs and deliver personalized, real-time assessments without coding makes them highly adaptable to clinical settings. This study underscores the potential of LLM-powered conversational artificial intelligence (AI) in health care and encourages further exploration of its use for real-time, disease risk assessment and decision-making support.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.37547\/tajiir\/volume08issue05-02","title":["Privacy-Preserving Generative AI for Legal CRM: Balancing Personalization with Compliance in the Legal and Professional Services Industry"],"author":[{"given":"Kush","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Product Manager, LexisNexis Inc"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,11]]},"container-title":["The American Journal of Interdisciplinary Innovations and Research"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.37547\/tajiir\/volume08issue05-02","abstract":"<jats:p>The fast usage of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in professional services Customer Relationship Management (CRM) systems has increased opportunities for hyper-personalized engagement with clients, but such change creates new risks in regulated practices such as legal services, where client data isn\u2019t just sensitive, it\u2019s protected by attorney\u2013client privilege and a thicket of compliance rules like GDPR and CCPA. Recent breaches of law firms' confidential client files owing to breaches of data privacy have already shown how messy this can get: the Proskauer Rose breach in 2023, for example, exposed sensitive deal documents, and Bryan Cave Leighton Paisner faced a similar crisis in 2024. Cases like these indicate the urgency for a generative AI framework that preserves privacy while synergistically maximizing the benefits of enhanced personalization.\n\nThis paper presents a privacy-preserving generative AI framework that is designed specifically for legal CRM scenarios. This idea is a multi-layered framework approach, which is differential privacy baked into the data, federated training so information doesn\u2019t have to leave its source, compliance checkpoints to catch GDPR\/CCPA gaps, and audit trails that hold systems accountable. A synthetic set of anonymized legal CRM records were produced to test the application of the framework. The results showed a 59% reduction in the exposure to privacy risk, a 40% improvement in compliance scores, three times more audibility, and acceptable levels of personalization relevance. In addition to the quantitative results, expert validation from legal technologists and compliance specialists for the adoption of frameworks\/case study's in practice was obtained.\n\nIn summary, this research offers three contributions: (1) this is the first research to focused on generating AI-driven personalization aligned to compliance-driven privacy safeguards for legal CRM; (2) this study offered a hybrid evaluation process that combines synthetic benchmarks with expert input for evaluation of adoption; and (3) this study contributes to shifting the conversation away from maximum personalization, irrespective of regulations\/standards and towards transparency, trust, and compliant, future proofed practices in a regulated domain of legal CRM.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5405948","title":["Why Federal Agencies Need Custom AI-Not Generic Models: How purpose-built AI and governance agents can reduce risk, ensure compliance, and improve performance in government operations"],"author":[{"given":"Mir Mohammed","family":"Assadullah","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5405948"},{"DOI":"10.63282\/3050-9416.ijaibdcms-v4i3p111","title":["AI Governance in Regulated Cloud-Native Insurance Platforms"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["International Journal of AI, BigData, Computational and Management Studies"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.63282\/3050-9416.ijaibdcms-v4i3p111"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-1-4842-9367-6_5","title":["Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Tom","family":"Taulli","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Generative AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-1-4842-9367-6_5"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/67363","title":["Generative Large Language Model\u2014Powered Conversational AI App for Personalized Risk Assessment: Case Study in COVID-19"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-2419-3101","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad Amin","family":"Roshani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0776-8045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiangyu","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2995-3385","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yao","family":"Qiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1717-7593","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Srinivasan","family":"Suresh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9579-6798","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Steven","family":"Hicks","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5183-0325","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Usha","family":"Sethuraman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0225-7817","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongxiao","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,27]]},"container-title":["JMIR AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/67363","abstract":"<jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Background<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated powerful capabilities in natural language tasks and are increasingly being integrated into health care for tasks like disease risk assessment. Traditional machine learning methods rely on structured data and coding, limiting their flexibility in dynamic clinical environments. This study presents a novel approach to disease risk assessment using generative LLMs through conversational artificial intelligence (AI), eliminating the need for programming.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Objective<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>This study evaluates the use of pretrained generative LLMs, including LLaMA2-7b and Flan-T5-xl, for COVID-19 severity prediction with the goal of enabling a real-time, no-code, risk assessment solution through chatbot-based, question-answering interactions. To contextualize their performance, we compare LLMs with traditional machine learning classifiers, such as logistic regression, extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and random forest, which rely on tabular data.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>We fine-tuned LLMs using few-shot natural language examples from a dataset of 393 pediatric patients, developing a mobile app that integrates these models to provide real-time, no-code, COVID-19 severity risk assessment through clinician-patient interaction. The LLMs were compared with traditional classifiers across different experimental settings, using the area under the curve (AUC) as the primary evaluation metric. Feature importance derived from LLM attention layers was also analyzed to enhance interpretability.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Generative LLMs demonstrated strong performance in low-data settings. In zero-shot scenarios, the T0-3b-T model achieved an AUC of 0.75, while other LLMs, such as T0pp(8bit)-T and Flan-T5-xl-T, reached 0.67 and 0.69, respectively. At 2-shot settings, logistic regression and random forest achieved an AUC of 0.57, while Flan-T5-xl-T and T0-3b-T obtained 0.69 and 0.65, respectively. By 32-shot settings, Flan-T5-xl-T reached 0.70, similar to logistic regression (0.69) and random forest (0.68), while XGBoost improved to 0.65. These results illustrate the differences in how generative LLMs and traditional models handle the increasing data availability. LLMs perform well in low-data scenarios, whereas traditional models rely more on structured tabular data and labeled training examples. Furthermore, the mobile app provides real-time, COVID-19 severity assessments and personalized insights through attention-based feature importance, adding value to the clinical interpretation of the results.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Conclusions<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Generative LLMs provide a robust alternative to traditional classifiers, particularly in scenarios with limited labeled data. Their ability to handle unstructured inputs and deliver personalized, real-time assessments without coding makes them highly adaptable to clinical settings. This study underscores the potential of LLM-powered conversational artificial intelligence (AI) in health care and encourages further exploration of its use for real-time, disease risk assessment and decision-making support.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>"},{"DOI":"10.5040\/9781509987023.ch-010","title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence-Powered Legal Technology: Application, Risk and Governance"],"author":[{"given":"Ran","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiuwen","family":"Yao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jiao","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Future-Proof Legal Services"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5040\/9781509987023.ch-010"},{"DOI":"10.2174\/9798898814984126010013","title":["Privacy-Preserving Federated Learning for Secure Deployment of Large Language Models in Healthcare and Financial Sectors"],"author":[{"given":"Shivangi","family":"Tyagi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technology, KIET Group of Institutions,\nDelhi-NCR, Ghaziabad, UP, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Raj","family":"Pandey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technology, KIET Group of Institutions,\nDelhi-NCR, Ghaziabad, UP, India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,26]]},"container-title":["Generative AI in Modern Healthcare"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2174\/9798898814984126010013","abstract":"<jats:p>Federated Learning (FL) is becoming more and more important in sectors\nlike healthcare and finance, where serious privacy and security issues arise from the\ncentralization of sensitive data, such as Electronic Health Records (EHR) and financial\ntransactions. FL, which was first presented by McMahan et al. (2017), allays these\nworries by sharing only model parameters and decentralizing model training\u2014raw data\nis not transferred. Techniques, like Secure Multi-party Computation (SMPC) and\nDifferential Privacy (DP), improve data protection throughout the learning process to\nfurther preserve privacy. FL has been effectively used for smaller models, but its use\nfor Large Language Models (LLMs) in privacy-sensitive fields is still in its infancy.\nThis study presents a privacy-preserving FL framework that integrates cryptographic\nmethods, including Homomorphic Encryption (HE), DP, and SMPC, and is designed\nfor the safe implementation of LLMs in the banking and healthcare industries. By\nallowing institutions to train LLMs on encrypted data locally, the proposed approach\nensures compliance with regulatory frameworks like GDPR and HIPAA. Federated\naveraging is used to aggregate encrypted model updates, and DP procedures make sure\nthat no sensitive information is exposed in the aggregated updates. Our methodology\ndemonstrated low performance degradation while maintaining strong privacy\nguarantees, achieving a test accuracy of 94.2% with an F1-score of 0.91 through\nextensive simulations utilizing real-world healthcare and financial datasets.\nAdditionally, it was found that applying gradient compression and optimum encryption\nsettings reduced transmission overhead by 25%. By tackling the major issues of\nprivacy, security, performance, and regulatory compliance, this work marks substantial\nprogress in the safe training and implementation of large-scale models in highly\nregulated industries.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4494272","title":["Gracenote.ai: Legal Generative AI for Regulatory Compliance"],"author":[{"given":"Jules","family":"Ioannidis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-0850-7681","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Joshua","family":"Harper","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ming Sheng","family":"Quah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dan","family":"Hunter","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4494272","abstract":"<jats:p>We investigate the transformative potential of large language models (LLMs) in the legal and regulatory compliance domain by developing advanced generative AI solutions, including a horizon scanning tool, an obligations generation tool, and an LLM-based expert system. Our approach combines the LangChain framework, OpenAI\u2019s GPT-4, text embeddings, and prompt engineering techniques to effectively reduce hallucinations and generate reliable and accurate domain specific outputs. A human-in-the-loop control mechanism is used as a final backstop to ensure accuracy and mitigate risk. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Our findings emphasise the role of LLMs as foundation engines in specialist tools and lay the groundwork for building the next generation of legal and compliance applications. Future research will focus on extending support across multiple jurisdictions and languages, refining prompts and text embedding datasets for enhanced legal reasoning capabilities, and developing autonomous AI agents and robust LLM-based expert systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7197703","title":["Navigating the AI Frontier in Public Administration: Generative AI, Agentic AI, and the Governance Imperative for Local Government"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-7027-3386","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Alfredo","family":"Prieto Pe","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7197703","abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of artificial intelligence into government operations is no longer a distant policy question, it is an immediate operational reality. Driven by massive industry investment and the commercial imperative to generate returns, technology vendors have embedded generative AI into the tools that public agencies already depend on, effectively rendering adoption a fait accompli, an outcome accepted by institutions that had little structural opportunity to deliberate otherwise. This paper examines the implications of this dynamic for local government across the United States, with particular attention to Texas, where the Texas Responsible AI Governance Act (TRAIGA, effective January 2026) establishes one of the nation's most significant state-level AI governance frameworks for public entities. Drawing on TRAIGA, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, and an analysis of AI integration by major government software vendors including Microsoft, Tyler Technologies, and Nutanix, the paper argues that local governments face a dual challenge: leveraging generative and agentic AI as tools for operational improvement while developing the governance capacity to manage the risks these technologies introduce. The paper concludes with recommendations for public administration educators and practitioners, with particular attention to the governance competencies that the next generation of public servants will require.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.33545\/26646501.2025.v7.i2a.168","title":["An interactive healthcare risk intelligence framework using explainable AI, predictive analytics, r shiny dashboards, and large language model governance"],"author":[{"given":"Temiloluwa Emmanuel","family":"Olabiyi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,1]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Molecular Biology and Biochemistry"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.33545\/26646501.2025.v7.i2a.168"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781118269213.ch36","title":["Public Sector Transparency\u2014How Is It Regulated in Europe?"],"author":[{"given":"Massimiliano","family":"Claps","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2008,2,29]]},"container-title":["Governance, Risk, and Compliance Handbook"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781118269213.ch36"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_8","title":["Managing and Addressing AI Compliance"],"author":[{"given":"James","family":"Sayles","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Principles of AI Governance and Model Risk Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_8"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6129286","title":["Explainable AI in U.S. Banking Fraud Detection: A Comparative Framework for Regulatory Compliance, Model Transparency, and Risk Governance"],"author":[{"given":"Mehedi","family":"Mostafa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6129286","abstract":"<jats:p>The increasing adoption of machine learning models in U.S. banking fraud detection has raised critical concerns regarding model transparency, regulatory compliance, and risk governance. While explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) techniques have been proposed to address these challenges, financial institutions continue to face uncertainty regarding the selection, evaluation, and governance of appropriate explanation methods across diverse fraud-detection use cases. This study presents a comparative and governance-oriented framework for explainable AI in U.S. banking systems, synthesizing model-agnostic and model-specific explanation techniques commonly used in fraud analytics. Building upon prior application-focused research on interpretable transaction modeling and agebased fraud analysis, this paper shifts the focus from individual implementations to a system-level evaluation of explainability methods under regulatory and operational constraints. The proposed framework evaluates explainable AI approaches across dimensions of transparency, stability, regulatory interpretability, bias awareness, and operational risk management. The study further discusses alignment with U.S. financial regulatory expectations and outlines practical guidance for responsible model deployment in fraud-detection environments. By providing a comparative and governance-centered perspective, this paper contributes a practical reference for financial institutions, policymakers, and analytics practitioners seeking to implement explainable AI solutions that balance predictive performance with accountability and regulatory compliance in U.S. banking fraud detection.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.32388\/a8dyj7","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments using DALL.E: Generative AI-Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dawood","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,25]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/a8dyj7","abstract":"<jats:p>This research investigated the role of artificial intelligence (AI), specifically the DALL.E model by OpenAI, in advancing data generation and visualization techniques in agriculture. DALL.E, an advanced AI image generator, works alongside ChatGPT's language processing to transform text descriptions and image clues into realistic visual representations of the content. The study used both approaches of image generation: text-to-image and image-to-image (variation). Six types of datasets depicting fruit crop environment were generated. These AI-generated images were then compared against ground truth images captured by sensors in real agricultural fields. The comparison was based on Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Feature Similarity Index (FSIM) metrics. The image-to-image generation exhibited a 5.78% increase in average PSNR over text-to-image methods, signifying superior image clarity and quality. However, this method also resulted in a 10.23% decrease in average FSIM, indicating a diminished structural and textural similarity to the original images. Similar to these measures, human evaluation also showed that images generated using image-to-image-based method were more realistic compared to those generated with text-to-image approach. The results highlighted DALL.E's potential in generating realistic agricultural image datasets and thus accelerating the development and adoption of imaging-based precision agricultural solutions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch3","title":["Future Approach Generative AI, Stylized Architecture, and its Potential in Finance"],"author":[{"given":"Abhinna Baxi","family":"Bhatnagar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Abhaya","family":"Nanad","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Anshul","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rakesh","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch3"},{"DOI":"10.30574\/wjarr.2025.25.3.0781","title":["AI-driven data governance in banking: Leveraging large language models for compliance and risk management"],"author":[{"family":"Rajesh Kamisetty","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Raj Nagamangalam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,30]]},"container-title":["World Journal of Advanced Research and Reviews"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.30574\/wjarr.2025.25.3.0781","abstract":"<jats:p>The banking sector has to deal with governance, compliance, and risk management challenges due to the evolving nature of the financial regulation and high volume of\u2002sensitive data. Real-time monitoring and anomaly detection are challenging in traditional rule based\u2002systems, which lead to inefficiencies and compliance risks. Using Large Language Models (LLMs), this paper discusses enabling banking data governance by automating compliance with banking regulations, risk assessment\u2002and fraud detection. Allow Intelligent data classification, predictive analytics and real-time auditing, in compliance with GDPR,\u2002Basel III, AML directive standards, etc. LLMs offer a transformative solution for secure and transparent financial operations, albeit with challenges like data privacy, model\u2002bias, explainability, etc. This research is based on real case studies\u2002and discusses how AI-based data governance can provide banks with improved security, compliance with regulatory mandates, and operational effectiveness<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch17","title":["AI\u2010Driven Generative Design Redefines the Engineering Process"],"author":[{"given":"Harpreet Kaur","family":"Channi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Amritjot","family":"Kaur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Surinder","family":"Kaur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch17"},{"DOI":"10.1111\/rmir.70041","title":["Generative AI adoption and barriers in the insurance industry"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-9074-5177","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Aisling","family":"Owen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Accounting and Finance Kemmy Business School, University of Limerick Limerick Ireland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4592-7558","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Barry","family":"Sheehan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Accounting and Finance Kemmy Business School, University of Limerick Limerick Ireland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1954-2630","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Martin","family":"Mullins","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Accounting and Finance Kemmy Business School, University of Limerick Limerick Ireland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,12]]},"container-title":["Risk Management and Insurance Review"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1111\/rmir.70041","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>This study examines the adoption of Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen AI) in the insurance industry through an institutional lens, drawing on semi\u2010structured interviews with 21 senior insurance professionals. While Gen AI offers significant potential for a data and text\u2010intensive industry, adoption remains cautious and uneven. Using qualitative thematic and content analysis, the study maps current and prospective Gen AI applications and identifies the barriers shaping their implementation. The findings show that Gen AI adoption in insurance is structured less by technical feasibility and more by institutional pressures related to regulation, professional norms, and trust. Insurers prioritize low\u2010risk, internally focused applications that align with dominant institutional logics of prudence, accountability, and risk control, with a continued reliance on human oversight, while higher\u2010stakes and customer\u2010facing use cases are adopted incrementally or deferred. Regulatory ambiguity emerges as a prominent constraint, encouraging strategies of anticipatory compliance and risk minimization. By situating Gen AI adoption within the institutional context of insurance, this study advances the literature on AI governance in regulated industries by showing how regulatory expectations, professional norms, and embedded risk logics actively shape where and how value from Gen AI is realized in practice. The study provides novel empirical insight into the uneven and selective nature of adoption across use cases and highlights how regulatory ambiguity may dampen beneficial innovation. In doing so, it underscores the importance of regulatory clarity, proportional risk\u2010based governance, and supervisory guidance in enabling responsible adoption, with implications for both policy and industry practice.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4811924","title":["Mitigating the Risks of Generative AI in Government through Algorithmic Governance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6047-7415","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mark","family":"Esposito","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Terence","family":"Tse","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4811924","abstract":"<jats:p>The launch of the generative artificial intelligence (gen AI) application ChatGPT by OpenAI launched artificial intelligence into public discourse and led to a wave of mass uptake of this technology in organizations in the private sector. At the same time, AI is increasingly incorporated into government functions and the public sector. We propose that governments and the public sector can set an example for the responsible use of AI technologies by following the principles of algorithmic governance traditionally recommended to the private sector. Algorithmic governance has traditionally been defined in the literature as governance by algorithms, or how artificial intelligence is used to make governance decisions and affect social ordering. However, we take an alternative approach; instead, we conceptualize algorithmic governance as the governance of algorithms. We begin by summarizing the risks of generative AI use in government, then outline algorithmic governance principles, a step-by-step approach to implementing algorithmic governance into government or public sector projects, opportunities for inter-sector collaboration, and final conclusions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6008674","title":["&lt;p&gt;AgentAssistX: An Agentic Generative AI Framework for Real-Time Life &amp;amp; LTC Insurance Advisory, Risk Scoring, and Compliance Validation in Cloud-Native Environments&lt;\/p&gt;"],"author":[{"given":"Pinak","family":"Mallick","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6008674","abstract":"<jats:p>Advanced, real-time risk assessment and advising solutions that enable transparency, compliance, and customer-focused decisionmaking are needed as LTC and life insurance markets become increasingly complex. Manual workflows, lack of personalization, and disconnected regulatory validation hamper traditional advice and underwriting. An AI platform called AgentAssistX may incorporate cloudnative capabilities, including automatic compliance validation, real-time policy advising, and dynamic risk rating. The mixed-methods study combined data from a Google Forms survey of insurance stakeholders (n=10 questions) and a secondary literature review on AI-driven insurance systems, agentic architectures, and cloud-native deployment strategies. Agentic AI and cloud-native infrastructures enable scalability, transparency, and reliability for regulatory insurance domains, according to research. AI-enhanced advising, risk grading, and automated regulatory checks are desired by survey respondents. AgentAssistX uses generic advisory agents, autonomous risk scorers, compliance validation agents, and a cloud-native orchestration layer for security, explainability, and modularity. The paper states that AgentAssistX can cover insurance technology gaps and enable regulatory sandbox trials, advanced explainable AI, and pilot testing.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6879502","title":["Experience Over Compliance: Why Frequent Use -Not Governance -Builds Managerial Trust in Generative AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-7764-7106","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Cheryl","family":"Cheng Xin Yi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6879502","abstract":"<jats:p>As a digital transformation leader working within one of Asia's most regulated financial environments, the author observed first-hand how organisations invested heavily in governance infrastructure as the primary response to GenAI adoption-and questioned whether that investment was producing the managerial confidence it was designed to build. That question became the foundation of this doctoral research, which sought to bring empirical rigour to a gap the author encountered not in the literature, but in practice. This working paper is derived from doctoral dissertation research completed at Golden Gate University. The full dissertation is available via ProQuest (forthcoming, June 2026). The author wishes to thank her dissertation supervisors, Dr. Ira Chatterjee and Dr. Bruce Wasserman, for their scholarly guidance throughout the research process, and the 100 managers who gave their time and candour as study participants.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/cfl.2024.4","title":["Navigating China\u2019s regulatory approach to generative artificial intelligence and large language models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7076-635X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mimi","family":"Zou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lu","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Cambridge Forum on AI: Law and Governance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cfl.2024.4","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>The rapid development of generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems, particularly those fuelled by increasingly advanced large language models, has raised concerns of their potential risks among policymakers globally. In July 2023, Chinese regulators enacted the Interim Measures for the Management of Generative AI Services (\u201cthe Measures\u201d). The Measures aim to mitigate various risks associated with public-facing generative AI services, particularly those concerning information content safety and security. China\u2019s approach to regulating AI to date has sought to address the risks associated with rapidly advancing AI technologies while fostering innovation and development. Tensions between these policy objectives are reflected in the provisions of the Measures. As Beijing moves towards establishing a comprehensive legal framework for AI governance, there will be growing interest in how China\u2019s approach may influence AI governance and regulation at a global level.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4872967","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments Using DALL.E: Generative AI-Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dawood","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4872967"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ijcnn64981.2025.11228800","title":["Advancing Social Governance through AI: A Large Language Model Approach to Government Response Analysis"],"author":[{"given":"Jie","family":"Teng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University,Institution of Government Data Resources,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Huanglan","family":"He","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University,Institution of Government Data Resources,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yiyang","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Jilin University,School of Business and Management,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tongshu","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"OnkoCare Life Science Technology (Suzhou) Co., Ltd,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Guangwei","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University,Institution of Government Data Resources,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,30]]},"container-title":["2025 International Joint Conference on Neural Networks (IJCNN)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ijcnn64981.2025.11228800"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4770726","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments using DALL.E: Generative AI- Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5417-6744","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4201-0621","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Dawood","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5337-4848","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4770726","abstract":"<jats:p>This research investigated the role of artificial intelligence (AI), specifically the DALLE model by OpenAI, in advancing data generation and visualization techniques in agriculture. DALLE, an advanced AI image generator, works alongside ChatGPT's language processing to transform text descriptions and image clues into realistic visual representations of the content. The study used both approaches of image generation: text-to-image and image-to image (variation). Six types of datasets depicting fruit crop environment were generated. These AI-generated images were then compared against ground truth images captured by sensors in real agricultural fields. The comparison was based on Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Feature Similarity Index (FSIM) metrics. The image-to-image generation exhibited a 5.78% increase in average PSNR over text-to-image methods, signifying superior image clarity and quality. However, this method also resulted in a 10.23% decrease in average FSIM, indicating a diminished structural and textural similarity to the original images. Similar to these measures, human evaluation also showed that images generated using image-to-image-based method were more realistic compared to those generated with text-to-image approach. The results highlighted DALLE's potential in generating realistic agricultural image datasets and thus accelerating the development and adoption of imaging-based precision agricultural solutions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch14","title":["Privacy Laws and Leak of Financial Data in the Era of Generative AI"],"author":[{"given":"Nitish Kumar","family":"Ojha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sanjeev","family":"Thakur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch14"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781118809181.ch8","title":["Legal Documentations in Islamic Finance"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2014,1,16]]},"container-title":["Shar\u012b`ah Non\u2010Compliance Risk Management and Legal Documentation in Islamic Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781118809181.ch8"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-2827-0_18","title":["Governance and Compliance in LLM Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Ankush","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Observability for Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-2827-0_18"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch10","title":["Role of Generative AI for Fraud Detection and Prevention"],"author":[{"given":"Prasanna","family":"Kulkarni","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Pankaj","family":"Pathak","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Samaya","family":"Pillai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Vishal","family":"Tigga","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch10"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch16","title":["Generative AI Tools for Product Design and Engineering"],"author":[{"given":"Manoj Singh","family":"Adhikari","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yogesh Kumar","family":"Verma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Sindhwani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shippu","family":"Sachdeva","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch16"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch7","title":["Generative AI"],"author":[{"given":"Rahul","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Krishna","family":"Pandey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Suman","family":"Kumari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch7"},{"DOI":"10.62311\/nesx\/rb5m-978-81-687299-5-7","title":["Quantum\u2013AI Legal Intelligence for Regulatory Compliance, Data Sovereignty, and Algorithmic Governance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-1023-7035","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Murali Krishna","family":"Pasupuleti","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National Education Services"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,30]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.62311\/nesx\/rb5m-978-81-687299-5-7","abstract":"<jats:p>Abstract:\nThis academic monograph develops a comprehensive framework for Quantum-AI legal intelligence across regulatory compliance, data sovereignty and algorithmic governance. It argues that contemporary legal systems increasingly confront decision environments shaped by automated models, platform infrastructures, cross-border data flows, post-quantum security risks and institutional demands for transparency. The manuscript conceptualises Quantum-AI not as a replacement for law, legal reasoning or public accountability, but as an advanced decision-support layer for optimization, uncertainty modelling, secure computation, evidentiary traceability and governance-aware analytics. Across five chapters, the book progresses from foundations and research design to causal explanation, trustworthy machine learning, sovereign data infrastructure and sectoral policy impact. It introduces conceptual diagrams, analytical tables, mathematical framing, evaluation metrics, risk frameworks and operational protocols that can guide researchers, regulators, legal technologists and policymakers. Global applicability is emphasised across South Asia, Europe, Africa and the Americas, with attention to public administration, regulated industries, data protection, digital public infrastructure and institutional readiness. The central contribution is a doctoral-level architecture for designing legal intelligence systems that are accurate, explainable, contestable, sovereignty-aware and resilient in the quantum era.\nKeywords\nQuantum-AI legal intelligence, regulatory compliance, data sovereignty, algorithmic governance, RegTech, legal analytics, trustworthy AI, legal MLOps, compliance automation, post-quantum security, confidential computing, explainable AI, causal inference, counterfactual evaluation, data lineage, audit trails, digital public infrastructure, cross-border data flows, public administration, model governance, risk scoring, human oversight, legal accountability, governance dashboards<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7217185","title":["Governance, Risk, and Trust in Generative AI-Enabled Information Systems: A Cross-Domain Framework for IT Strategy, Project Management, and Healthcare Data Security"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4300-5058","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Samuel","family":"Babatunde","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muiz","family":"Shaw","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7217185","abstract":"<jats:p>As generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) becomes embedded in enterprise and clinical information systems, governance frameworks originally designed for deterministic software are proving inadequate for systems whose behavior evolves after deployment. Existing research on GenAI in IT strategy, project management, and healthcare information systems documents productivity, integration, and care-delivery benefits, but rarely integrates the governance, risk, and trust requirements that determine whether these benefits can be realized safely and at scale. This paper develops a cross-domain conceptual framework describing four governance dimensions, risk classification, data-boundary control, human oversight, and regulatory alignment relevant to GenAI-enabled information systems in IT strategy, project management, and healthcare data security. Synthesizing established governance frameworks (including NIST AI RMF's Generative AI Profile and Gartner's AI Trust, Risk, and Security Management model), healthcare-specific data-protection literature, and the four domain studies motivating this inquiry, the framework is applied through disclosed, non-empirical proof-of-concept scenarios. The analysis does not report primary empirical, security-incident, or clinical data. The paper discusses theoretical, managerial, and policy implications for organizations governing GenAI-enabled systems, with particular attention to the elevated regulatory stakes of healthcare data security, and proposes an agenda for empirical validation.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.70715\/jitcai.2025.v2.i2.022","title":["MACHINE LEARNING (ML) TO EVALUATE GOVERNANCE, RISK, AND COMPLIANCE (GRC) RISKS ASSOCIATED WITH LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS (LLMs)"],"author":[{"given":"Upakar","family":"Bhatta","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/049c8eh51","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Central Washington University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information Technology, Cybersecurity, and Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70715\/jitcai.2025.v2.i2.022","abstract":"<jats:p>In today\u2019s AI-driven digital world, Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) has become vital for organizations as they leverage AI technologies to drive business success and resilience. GRC represents a strategic approach that helps organization using Large Language Models (LLMs) automation tasks and enhances customer service, while maintaining the regulatory complexity across various industries and regions. This paper explores a machine learning approach to evaluate Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) risks associated with Large Language Models (LLMs). It utilizes Azure OpenAI Service logs to construct a representative dataset, with key features including\u00a0 response_time_ms, model_type, temperature, tokens_used, is_logged, data_sensitivity, compliance_flag, bias_score, and toxicity_score. These features are used\u00a0to train a model that predicts GRC risk levels in LLM interactions, enabling organizations to improve efficiency, foster innovation, and deliver customer value, while maintaining compliance and regulatory requirements.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6624698","title":["An Enterprise AI Governance Maturity Model for Cloud-deployed Generative AI Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-5523-4401","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rahul","family":"Modi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6624698","abstract":"<jats:p>Scalable cloud services, built-in security tooling, and accelerating competition are the factors behind the rapid enterprise integration of Generative AI into Microsoft Azure ecosystems (Ettinger; Biswas). Organizations are starting to use Azure-native GenAI to make their operations more productive, improve analytics, and engage with customers, and address emerging governance and risk management challenges (Biswas; Schiros et al.). It is a case of the creation of a systematic AI governance maturity model with a specific focus on Azure-based Generative AI implementations (Wairagade and Govindarajan; Rangan and Yoost). It was intended to have the enterprise goals to match the patterns of the Azure architecture, platform services, and realities of operations in various organizational settings (Rangan and Yoost; Ettinger). The methodology is a literature review and synthesis of enterprise architecture literature, developed governance frameworks, and operational practice of cloud GenAI (Denni-Fiberesima; Marchena Sekli and De La Vega). Integration of strategic, technical and organizational perspectives was done through comparative analysis to create a coherent and action-oriented maturity structure (Denni-Fiberesima; Wairagade and Govindarajan). The most important observations show that there are six dimensions of core governance capabilities, namely: strategy, architecture, security, compliance, operations and value realization (MODEL, 2024; Kongsten and Kathirgamadas). These dimensions indicate common enterprise pain points in large scale Azure GenAI deployments and regulatory landscapes (Kongsten and Kathirgamadas; Biswas). The maturity model categorizes capabilities into stages of progression supporting the controlled experimentation, standardized deployment, and optimization on an enterprise-wide level (MODEL, 2024; Rangan and Yoost). The main finding is that a progressive model of governance maturity makes it possible to adopt Generative AI in an Azure environment in a scaled, secure, and responsible way (Biswas; Schiros et al.).<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5551194","title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Future of Accounting Competencies: Quantitative Evidence from the Large Language Model (LLM) Revolution"],"author":[{"given":"Dulani","family":"Jayasuriya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5551194"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-030-64254-9_10","title":["AI and Legal Issues"],"author":[{"given":"Georgios I.","family":"Zekos","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2021]]},"container-title":["Economics and Law of Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-030-64254-9_10"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch22","title":["Harnessing Generative AI for Engineering and Product Design"],"author":[{"family":"Sakshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chetan","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gunjan","family":"Verma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nisha","family":"Chanana","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch22"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v2","title":["Imperfectly Perfect AI Chatbots: Limitations of Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v2","abstract":"<jats:p>Generative AI has transformed the landscape of AI chatbots in a way that they are increasingly becoming human-like in their understanding and working. Generative AI is used for creating new contents, and most modern AI chatbots are designed using two types of generative AI models: Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Model (LMMs). LLMs deal with the text, whereas LMMs deal with more modalities including text, image, audio and video. This continuous enhancement of generative AI models is bringing AI chatbots closer to the human working. Nonetheless, these AI chatbots are not perfect, as they have several limitations, and their users should comprehend before fully relying on these imperfectly perfect AI chatbots. Depending on the type of use of AI chatbots, these limitations may or may not impact them significantly. This paper will organise the limitations of AI chatbots into six main categories: intelligence and understanding related limitations; accuracy and credibility related limitations; ethics and regulations related limitations; accountability, transparency and consistency related limitations; design, coding and training related limitations; and human, machine and vender related limitations. The aim of this paper is to emphasise, organise and analyse numerous limitations of AI chatbots into the proposed categories; accordingly, users should become more cognisant about all these limitations in deciding their suitability or unsuitability for the specific use cases.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v1","title":["Imperfectly Perfect AI Chatbots: Limitations of Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Generative AI has transformed the landscape of AI chatbots in a way that they are increasingly becoming human-like in their understanding and working. Generative AI is used for creating new contents, and most modern AI chatbots are designed using two types of generative AI models: Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Model (LMMs). LLMs deal with the text, whereas LMMs deal with more modalities including text, image, audio and video. This continuous enhancement of generative AI models is bringing AI chatbots closer to the human working. Nonetheless, these AI chatbots are not perfect, as they have several limitations, and their users should comprehend before fully relying on these imperfectly perfect AI chatbots. Depending on the type of use of AI chatbots, these limitations may or may not impact them significantly. This paper will organise the limitations of AI chatbots into six main categories: intelligence and understanding related limitations; accuracy and credibility related limitations; ethics and regulations related limitations; accountability, transparency and consistency related limitations; design, coding and training related limitations; and human, machine and vender related limitations. The aim of this paper is to emphasise, organise and analyse numerous limitations of AI chatbots into the proposed categories; accordingly, users should become more cognisant about all these limitations in deciding their suitability or unsuitability for the specific use cases.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1787\/42815683-en","title":["Generative AI experimentation in government"],"author":[{"name":"OECD","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,20]]},"container-title":["OECD Working Papers on Public Governance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1787\/42815683-en"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch2","title":["Automating Data Entry in the Indian Banking Industry Through Generative AI"],"author":[{"given":"Srividya","family":"Prathiba","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Rahul","family":"Pandey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yashwant","family":"Patel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manjinder","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch2"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173121409.91676949\/v1","title":["Applications of AI Chatbots Based on Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173121409.91676949\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>AI chatbots are progressively becoming more human-like in their creativity and intelligence. Predominantly, AI chatbots based on generative AI models such as Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Model (LMMs) are a significant breakthrough in AI, as they have become more accurate, creative, context-aware, and can better mimic human-like interactions. Generative AI is an umbrella term for all AI technologies used to generate contents, where LLMs can process and generate textual data, and LMMs can process and generate multiple types of data modalities which are also known as multimodal AI models. AI chatbots and multimodal AI models offer several benefits, such as speed, accuracy, creativity, efficiency, and versatility; therefore, they can be used in nearly every sector such as a content creator, proactive assistant, reliable companion, customer assistant, and business operations facilitator. Nonetheless, these AI chatbots and multimodal AI models have some limitations, specially, inaccuracy and hallucination; that can affect and limit their usage for any critical task in any application area. Therefore, human verification and supervision is required for its reliable, safe and guided use. Moreover, their use in any application area also requires appropriate ethical consideration and regulatory compliance, alongside the human verification of generated responses. This paper will present twenty-two applications of AI chatbots and multimodal AI models, which are organised into two categories: personal applications and organisational applications. Subsequently, it will also describe the crucial ethical consideration and regulatory compliance for each application area to emphasise its related ethics, regulations and other risks for humans.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/books978-3-7258-7704-1","title":["Generative AI and Large Language Models"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/books978-3-7258-7704-1"},{"DOI":"10.52783\/pmj.v35.i1s.7170","title":["Leveraging Large Language Models for Automated Clinical Documentation and Decision Support within Generative AI Healthcare Frameworks"],"author":[{"family":"Venubabu kommalapati","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,13]]},"container-title":["Panamerican Mathematical Journal"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52783\/pmj.v35.i1s.7170","abstract":"<jats:p>The research paper investigates how large language models (LLMs) can improve healthcare systems which use generative AI to automate clinical documentation and decision support systems. Intelligent automation solutions become essential for healthcare organizations because their administrative tasks have grown which enables physicians to access precise medical data whenever required. LLMs apply their advanced natural language processing abilities to transform unstructured clinical information into structured medical records which help physicians decrease their documentation tasks and avoid mistakes in record-keeping.The proposed framework uses LLMs to deliver context-based clinical decision support through patient data analysis which includes medical histories and current data to create evidence-based recommendations. The system enables electronic health record (EHR) integration while it protects data privacy and security through its compliance with established standards. The system needs model transparency which should work together with explainability and bias mitigation features to establish trustworthy clinical systems that produce dependable results.The testing results show that the system achieves better documentation accuracy and faster processing times while making better decisions when compared to standard techniques. The study shows that LLMs improve healthcare productivity through their application in generative AI healthcare systems which use data-driven medical decisions to create better patient treatment methodologies.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/b978-0-443-33592-1.00004-8","title":["Large language models in interpreting legal documents"],"author":[{"given":"Simone","family":"Corbo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Challenges and Applications of Generative Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/b978-0-443-33592-1.00004-8"},{"DOI":"10.14293\/pr2199.002326.v1","title":["Strategic Adoption of Generative AI in U.S. Manufacturing: Balancing Innovation Acceleration with Trade, Compliance, and Intellectual Property Governance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-7063-9086","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lawal","family":"G. Anand","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,12]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14293\/pr2199.002326.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>\n                  <jats:bold>\n                    <jats:bold>The integration of generative AI into U.S. manufacturing environments introduces both innovation potential and regulatory complexity. This paper proposes a compliance-aware architecture for deploying generative AI models within industrial design and production workflows. The framework defines technical control layers, including model access governance, embedded compliance screening, and data-classification mechanisms to prevent model leakage, safeguard intellectual property, and ensure adherence to U.S. export control regulations. Unlike prior studies focused solely on AI performance or policy analysis, this work outlines a system-level implementation model combining generative design automation, secure model orchestration, and risk-aware data pipelines. Through scenario-based modeling and workflow analysis, the study demonstrates how manufacturers can accelerate innovation while maintaining governance integrity across digital supply chains. The proposed architecture bridges AI engineering, cybersecurity, and regulatory design, enabling responsible adoption of generative AI in complex manufacturing ecosystems.<\/jats:bold>\n                  <\/jats:bold>\n                <\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2118\/221872-ms","title":["Generative AI and Large Language Model Assisted Causal Discovery and Inference for Driving Process Improvements"],"author":[{"given":"P.","family":"Rai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Baker Hughes, Houston, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Jain","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Baker Hughes, Houston, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Anand","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Baker Hughes, Houston, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,4]]},"container-title":["ADIPEC"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2118\/221872-ms","abstract":"<jats:p>Data-driven process management coupled with machine learning have been successful in driving commercial value to oil and gas operators by offering insights into process disruptions and their root causes. One frequently used approach is to analyze causes of process disruptions exclusively from historical data. In general, specific insights in the form of high correlation between certain process performance indicators and a well-defined measure of production inefficiency is often confounded as responsible causal factors. While this may yield some insights, the complexity of processes, measured in terms of number of entities involved and their interrelationships, requires a more nuanced approach that must include the context of the specific process. Thus, data analysis must be augmented with significant inputs from experts. Causal Inference provides a conceptual framework and tools for doing such analysis.<\/jats:p>\n               <jats:p>In causal analysis, we embed this specific knowledge of subject matter experts using causal graphs consisting of process features (nodes) and their dependency (directed edges). For complex processes however, constructing causal graphs could be non-trivial due to ambiguity over which nodes to include and the plausible direction of their relationships. With the advent of foundational Large Language Models (LLM), there is an opportunity to mitigate this problem by utilizing the enormous information it encodes. Tools and technologies now exist to customize the response of LLM using retrieval of information from a corpus of specific high-quality knowledge in the form of related literature and data. It can therefore be used to assist the domain expert in building and finetuning the causal graph, and in simpler cases, can completely automate this step.<\/jats:p>\n               <jats:p>In this work, we propose a two-step approach to combine the power of LLMs and Causal Analysis for analyzing inefficiencies in production processes. In the first step, we implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) enhanced LLM prompting on a curated dataset designed to answer specific questions on relationship between process performance indicators. The outcome of this step is a directed acyclic graph encoding dependency of process performance indicators. Domain experts can validate or potentially refine the LLM-generated causal graph based on their domain knowledge for eliminating spurious hallucinations. In the second step, we use an appropriate causal inference method on the refined causal diagram and historical production data to estimate the causal effect of process variable contributing to disruptions or inefficiencies. Thus, by combining human expertise with machine learning, this framework offers a comprehensive approach for optimizing production processes.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7127098","title":["Cognitive Drift in Generative AI: Self-Normalized Displacement as a Measurement Principle for Semantic Coherence in Large Language Model Outputs"],"author":[{"given":"Tatu","family":"Lertola","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7127098","abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Hallucination in large language models (LLMs) has been predominantly treated as a surface-level error: factually incorrect content to be filtered post-generation. We propose a reframing: hallucination as loss of internal orientation, a systemic phenomenon rooted in drift, stagnation, and overconfident generation under narrowing awareness. We introduce the Volatility Factor (VF), a trajectory-level coherence signal, and the Stagnation Signal (Sg), which detects generation that continues without directional progress. Both are computed from embedding geometry alone, requiring no access to model internals.&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Beyond the framework, we identify and provide initial empirical support for a candidate measurement principle: self-normalized displacement. Behavioral integrity, we propose, cannot be assessed on an absolute scale; it is measurable only as displacement from the system's own established baseline. The same normalization that resolves per-request prompt-injection detection in our prior Intent Vectoring work (the deviation ratio) also resolves trajectory-level drift detection (the displacement ratio): in a controlled multi-turn evaluation (4 conversation tracks x 2 open-weight models, 100 turns), absolute signal levels failed to separate drifting from aligned conversations, while the displacement ratio classified all eight series correctly, with an online variant detecting both drift series at zero false positives. We report the formula's full evolution including two instructive failures, recalibrate Sg for multi-turn settings, and present a calibrated phase detection grid. The framework positions per-request, per-session, and prospectively per-lifetime monitoring as instances of one measurement principle at three timescales. We present the principle as a working hypothesis, the instrument as functional but unrefined, and devote explicit sections to the maturity stage of the research and to what the instrument structurally cannot see.&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394250226.ch31","title":["Navigating the Cyber Insurance Maze"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,29]]},"container-title":["The Cybersecurity Guide to Governance, Risk, and Compliance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394250226.ch31"},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783111425078-001","title":["1 Unveiling the Power of Generative AI: A Journey into Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"A.","family":"Ashwini","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"J.","family":"Jency Rubia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"H.","family":"Sehina","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"B.","family":"Sundaravadivazhagan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,9]]},"container-title":["Generative AI and LLMs"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783111425078-001"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781118809181.ch3","title":["The Major Risk Elements of\n            <i>Shar\u012b`ah<\/i>\n            Non\u2010Compliance Risk and Its Causes in Islamic Finance Operations"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2014,1,16]]},"container-title":["Shar\u012b`ah Non\u2010Compliance Risk Management and Legal Documentation in Islamic Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781118809181.ch3"},{"DOI":"10.31228\/osf.io\/bheqw","title":["Generative AI Large Language Models and Researching the Law"],"author":[{"given":"Paul Douglas","family":"Callister","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,16]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31228\/osf.io\/bheqw","abstract":"<p>Generative AI Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly influencing the legal research landscape, challenging the traditional cognitive authority within the profession. This article explores how these models, despite their potential to simplify the vast amount of legal information, may also introduce risks of misinformation due to AI \"hallucinations\" and the advantages and limitations of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems. By examining various legal research problems, the article highlights both the capabilities and shortcomings of generative AI in legal contexts. It emphasizes the need for vigilance, critical thinking, and a continued reliance on traditional research methods to mitigate the risks associated with over-reliance on AI-generated content. As the legal profession confronts an overwhelming influx of information, the role of AI in legal research will likely grow, making it crucial to balance cognitive ease with accuracy and reliability.<\/p>"}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}